In this lesson we'll look at what volume really means. If area tells you how much flat surface a shape covers, volume tells you how much space a three-dimensional object takes up, and we measure it in cubic units, like centimetres cubed or metres cubed. Picture a tiny cube that is one centimetre on every side, what we call a unit cube. If you stacked those cubes inside a solid and counted them, that count is the volume. A cube that is three centimetres on each side fits three by three by three, which is twenty-seven unit cubes, giving a volume of twenty-seven centimetres cubed. A box measuring four by two by five centimetres holds four times two times five, which is forty unit cubes. The general rule for a rectangular box is just length times width times height, so remember to make sure every measurement is in the same unit before you multiply, otherwise convert first. Finally, don't confuse volume with capacity. Volume is the space inside the shape, while capacity is how much liquid it can hold, often expressed in litres. Now let's see it in action.